VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HMO-022
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of severe, atypical chest pain and palpitations in a patient with multi-organ dysfunction, requiring urgent evaluation to rule out acute arrhythmias or heart failure.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed a patient with a recent diagnosis of "bronze diabetes" experiencing sudden, irregular heartbeats and new shortness of breath at rest. Urgent in-person evaluation was necessary to assess for new-onset arrhythmias or restrictive cardiomyopathy, as delaying assessment until normal clinic hours posed a high risk of fatal cardiac decompensation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My heart is fluttering wildly in my chest, I can't catch my breath, and my knuckles have been aching terribly all week."
History of Present Illness: The 50-year-old patient reports a 12-month insidious history of progressive profound generalized fatigue and systemic lethargy. They have noted chronic arthralgia predominantly affecting the second and third metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joints. They were recently diagnosed with new-onset diabetes mellitus and have noticed a generalized slate-grey/bronze hyperpigmentation of their skin. Over the past 4 hours, they developed sudden, rapid palpitations, a sensation of chest fullness, and mild dyspnoea at rest. They also endorse right upper quadrant abdominal discomfort and a complete loss of libido over the past year.
Functional Status: Currently resting in a recliner; breathless upon walking to the front door.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of syncope if the arrhythmia degrades into a lethal rhythm.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/70 | HR: 135 (Irregularly Irregular) | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, visibly anxious adult. Notable bronze hyperpigmentation of the sun-exposed skin on the arms and face.
Physical Exam:
Cardiovascular: Tachycardic with an irregularly irregular rhythm, consistent with new-onset Atrial Fibrillation. No murmurs auscultated. JVP is elevated at 3 cm above the sternal angle.
Respiratory: Mild bibasal fine crackles heard on auscultation.
Abdomen: Soft, but significant firm hepatomegaly is palpable 4 cm below the right costal margin, with mild localized tenderness.
Musculoskeletal: Bony enlargement and tenderness noted over the 2nd and 3rd MCP joints bilaterally.
Environmental Observations: Blood glucose monitor and recent insulin pens on the table.
Medication Reconciliation: Recently commenced on Metformin and long-acting insulin. No anti-arrhythmics.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Haemochromatosis - Status: Exacerbated (New-Onset Atrial Fibrillation/Early Cardiomyopathy)
Differentials:

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the absence of classic crushing, radiating chest pain and the presence of an irregular rhythm in the context of known systemic iron overload, pointing toward structural/electrical pathology rather than pure ischemia.

Hyperthyroidism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the classic presentation of bronze diabetes, hepatomegaly, and specific MCP arthralgia which defines haemochromatosis, though a TSH should be checked in the hospital.

Rheumatoid Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the multi-organ systemic involvement (diabetes, liver, heart, skin) and the specific targeting of the 2nd/3rd MCP joints (classic for iron deposition) rather than symmetrical PIP/wrist synovitis.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits the classic tetrad of advanced haemochromatosis: bronze skin, diabetes, hepatomegaly, and arthralgia. The acute presentation of rapid, irregularly irregular palpitations and bibasal crackles strongly indicates iron deposition in the myocardium causing new-onset Atrial Fibrillation and early restrictive cardiomyopathy. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would expose the patient to uncontrolled rapid ventricular rates, worsening pulmonary congestion, and a high risk of thromboembolic stroke from the new arrhythmia. The urgent home visit was essential to physically diagnose the arrhythmia, assess haemodynamic stability, and arrange immediate cardiological escalation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department for a 12-lead ECG, cardiac monitoring, and urgent rate control. Outpatient follow-up will require urgent iron studies (anticipating elevated transferrin saturation >45% and high ferritin) and genetic testing for the HFE mutation (C282Y) to confirm the underlying cause once the cardiac crisis is stabilized.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Remain seated in the recliner and avoid all physical exertion tonight. By minimizing your body's oxygen demand, you prevent your racing, inefficient heart from failing further and causing fluid to build up in your lungs.

Completely avoid any vitamin C supplements or iron-fortified cereals. By strictly limiting dietary iron and its absorption enhancers, you prevent throwing more fuel on the fire of this systemic iron overload condition.
